## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge caching behavior, tail latency, and backpressure under regional surge—a practical beginner scenario for performance testing.

## Key Concepts
- Edge caching and cache warming
- Tail latency (p95/p99)
- API gateway backpressure and circuit breakers
- Regional autoscaling and cost/latency trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = {
  scenarios: {
    regionalSurge: { executor: 'ramping-vus', startVUs: 0, stages: [ { duration: '2m', target: 50 }, { duration: '8m', target: 200 } ] }
  }
};
export default function () {
  http.get('https://region-A.example.com/pricing');
  http.post('https://region-A.example.com/book', JSON.stringify({}), { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  sleep(0.5);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would network jitter influence your test results and how would you compensate for it?
- What signals would you use to decide if the edge cache warming was successful?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for real-time collaboration reveal tail latency, backpressure handling, and cross-region performance, crucial for high-scale video platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency across microservices
- End-to-end tracing and replay
- Backpressure and autoscaling
- Network emulation across mobile/desktop

## Code Example
```javascript
// k6 load test example
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep, check } from 'k6';
export let options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 200 },
    { duration: '5m', target: 200 },
    { duration: '2m', target: 0 }
  ],
  thresholds: { 'http_req_duration': ['p(95)<500'] }
};
export default function () {
  const payload = JSON.stringify({ userId: __VU, stroke: { id: Math.random().toString(36).slice(2), x: Math.random(), y: Math.random(), color: '#0af' } });
  const res = http.post('https://api.example.com/stroke', payload, { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(0.1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you instrument end-to-end latency and replay traces to identify bottlenecks?
- How would you validate backpressure strategies when bursts scale beyond provisioned capacity?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-region, serverless + Kubernetes orchestration, backpressure handling, and failure modes under bursty input.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency, tail latency, cold starts, backpressure, autoscaling, failure scenarios, observability.

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = { stages: [ {duration: '5m', target: 5000}, {duration: '5m', target: 5000}, {duration: '5m', target: 0} ] };
export default function () {
  const payload = JSON.stringify({ videoSize: Math.random() * 100 });
  let res = http.post('https://ingest.example.com/upload', payload, { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }});
  check(res, { 'status was 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(0.2);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure cold-start impact in production without skewing costs?
- How would you validate global autoscaling across regions?
